## 5. DATABASE RULES

### 5.1 Core Tables (TEMPLATE — never modify)
- `roles` — system roles: super_admin, admin, user, guest (is_system=true, cannot be deleted)
- `users` — one role per user (role_id FK to roles). Single role model. Buyers extend if needed.
- `oauth_accounts` — one user can have multiple OAuth providers linked
- `audit_logs` — login, logout, signup, role_change, password_reset events

### 5.2 BaseModel Mixin — All Models Must Extend This
Every SQLAlchemy model (template and buyer) extends `BaseModel` from `backend/db/base.py`.
This gives every table: `id` (UUID, PK), `created_at` (timestamptz), `updated_at` (timestamptz).
Never define id, created_at, or updated_at manually in any model — they come from BaseModel.

### 5.3 Buyer Models
- Live in `backend/business/{feature}/models.py`
- Extend `BaseModel` always
- Use UUID primary keys always
- Reference `users.id` with a `user_id` FK when data is user-owned

### 5.4 Migrations
- Dev: `detect_unapplied.py` auto-generates and applies migrations. Print a warning after.
- Prod: `check_prod_migrations.py` BLOCKS startup if unapplied migrations exist. Exit code 1.
- Never manually write SQL migration files — always use `alembic revision --autogenerate`
- Seed script (`seed.py`) is idempotent — uses `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` logic

### 5.5 Redis Key Patterns — Always Use Exactly These
```
session:{user_id}           → Hash, TTL 7 days, fields: access_token, refresh_token, role, email, full_name
blacklist:{token_jti}       → String "1", TTL = remaining token lifetime
verify:{verification_token} → String user_id, TTL 24 hours
reset:{reset_token}         → String user_id, TTL 1 hour
```
Never invent new Redis key patterns without the phase instruction specifying them.

