## 9. BACKEND CODE RULES

### 9.1 FastAPI Style
- Async/await everywhere. No synchronous database calls.
- All endpoints must have a Pydantic `response_model`.
- Never return raw Python dicts as responses — always go through a Pydantic schema.
- Use `Depends(get_current_user)` on all protected endpoints.
- Use `@require_roles([...])` on all role-gated endpoints.
- All routers use a `prefix` and `tags` in `APIRouter(prefix="/...", tags=["..."])`.

### 9.2 SQLAlchemy
- Use SQLAlchemy 2.x async patterns.
- All models extend `BaseModel` from `backend/db/base.py`.
- Use `get_db` dependency for database sessions.
- Never create raw SQL strings — use SQLAlchemy ORM methods.

### 9.3 Business Router Auto-Registration
- `main.py` auto-discovers all packages in `backend/business/` and registers their `router`.
- Buyer never edits `main.py`. Their router is picked up automatically on restart.
- A business feature folder must have `router.py` with a variable named `router`.

### 9.4 Email Service
- Always use `email_service.send(to, subject, html)` from `backend/mailer/service.py`.
- Never import email provider adapters directly in business logic.
- Provider is selected automatically via `EMAIL_PROVIDER` env var.

### 9.5 Configuration
- All env vars are read through `backend/core/config.py` (pydantic-settings).
- Never use `os.environ.get()` directly in application code — always use `settings.VAR_NAME`.
- If an env var is missing or invalid, `config.py` raises a clear error on startup.

### 9.6 Python Package Naming — Never Shadow stdlib or PyPI
- Before creating a new package folder in `backend/`, check if the name conflicts with:
  - Python standard library modules (`email`, `json`, `http`, `sys`, `os`, `pathlib`, etc.)
  - Installed PyPI packages (`redis`, `sqlalchemy`, `fastapi`, `jose`, etc.)
- If conflict exists, rename the package. Examples:
  - `backend/email/` → `backend/mailer/` (conflicts with stdlib `email`)
  - `backend/redis/` → `backend/cache/` (conflicts with PyPI `redis`)
- Always verify imports work after creating a new package.

